thm-6-1-13.tex:
\leanmarker{thm-6-1-13}{Theorem 6.1.13} Free strict dcpo- and domain-algebras exist. Equivalently, the forgetful functors
\[
\begin{array}{rclcrcl}
\mathbf{DCPO}_{\bot!}(\Sigma,E)&\longrightarrow&\mathbf{DCPO}_{\bot!},&&
\mathbf{CONT}_{\bot!}(\Sigma,E)&\longrightarrow&\mathbf{CONT}_{\bot!},\\
\mathbf{ALG}_{\bot!}(\Sigma,E)&\longrightarrow&\mathbf{ALG}_{\bot!}
\end{array}
\]
have left adjoints.

p87-b3.tex:
None of the categories of approximated dcpos or domains considered so far is complete, so the Adjoint Functor Theorem cannot be used directly. We write \(\mathbf{CONT}(\Sigma,E)\) for dcpo-algebras whose carriers are continuous domains, and suppress the forgetful functor \(U:\mathbf{CONT}(\Sigma,E)\to\mathbf{CONT}\). The required adjointness condition is the following universal property:
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.92, >=stealth]
  \node (D)  at (0,1.65) {$D$};
  \node (FD) at (3.0,1.65) {$F(D)$};
  \node (A)  at (3.0,-0.9) {$A$};
  \draw[->] (D) -- node[above] {$\eta$} (FD);
  \draw[->] (D) -- node[below left] {$g$} (A);
  \draw[->] (FD) -- node[right] {$\operatorname{ext}(g)$} (A);
  \node[font=\bfseries] at (-1.1,-0.1) {CONT};
  \draw[dashed] (4.35,2.15) -- (4.35,-1.35);
  \node (FDa) at (5.65,1.65) {$\underline{F(D)}$};
  \node (Aa)  at (5.65,-0.9) {$\underline{A}$};
  \draw[->] (FDa) -- node[right] {$\exists!\operatorname{ext}(g)$} (Aa);
  \node[font=\bfseries] at (7.55,-0.1) {CONT$(\Sigma,E)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}
Thus for every continuous domain \(D\) one must construct a dcpo-algebra \(F(D)\), a continuous map \(\eta:D\to F(D)\), and for every algebra \(A\) and continuous \(g:D\to A\) a unique homomorphism \(\operatorname{ext}(g):F(D)\to A\) with \(\operatorname{ext}(g)\circ\eta=g\). The construction works with a fixed basis \(\langle B,\ll\rangle\) of \(D\). The underlying set before quotienting is \(FB=T_\Sigma(B)\). On \(FB\) define the substitutive preorder \(\sqsubseteq\) from the inequations by
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
(A1)& t\sqsubseteq t,\\
(A2)& s\sqsubseteq t\quad\text{if this is an instance of an inequation from }E,\\
(R1)& s_i\sqsubseteq t_i\ (1\le i\le n)\Rightarrow f(s_1,\ldots,s_n)\sqsubseteq f(t_1,\ldots,t_n),\\
(R2)& s\sqsubseteq t\text{ and }t\sqsubseteq u\Rightarrow s\sqsubseteq u.
\end{array}
\]
The associated equivalence relation is written \(\sim\), and the factor \(FB/{\sim}\) is ordered by \(\sqsubseteq\).

