The following exercises ask the reader to check related failures and constructions. In particular, for the usual equational theories of groups or Boolean algebras, any dcpo-algebra is trivially ordered, so the free construction collapses each connected component of the generating dcpo. For a reduct \(\langle\Sigma,E\rangle\) of \(\langle\Sigma',E'\rangle\), the induced forgetful functor from \(C(\Sigma',E')\) to \(C(\Sigma,E)\) has a left adjoint by the same methods as Theorems 6.1.2 and 6.1.7. The exercises also ask about freely completing partial domain algebras, compactness of values of operations on compact elements, and examples showing that Plotkin powerdomains need not remain in \(\mathbf{F\text{-}B}\) or be \(L\)-domains.

Exercise 11 constructs an algebraic domain \(D\) for which Theorem 6.2.19 cannot be extended. The compact elements are arranged in finite sets \(M_\alpha\) indexed by finite subsets \(\alpha\subseteq\mathbb{R}\), ordered so that \(M_\alpha\ll_{EM}M_\beta\) when \(\alpha\subseteq\beta\). Each \(M_\alpha\) has one white element and \(|\alpha|!\) black elements, arranged in layers as in Figure 14.

